## Complex: a crate that runs its own threads (tokio-backed)

What it shows: an async Rust API whose futures become Jet wait points; a `Send + 'static` callback that the crate
calls from its own worker threads, delivered on the owning task; `Drop` as `Close`.

```jet
package: {
    name: "feeds"
    deps: { feedkit: rust@"feedkit@0.4" }       // a tokio-based RSS client
}

use fk: rust.feedkit

Counter: struct {
    seen: Int
}

run: fn() {
    client: fk.Client.builder().timeout(5s).build()         // owns a tokio runtime; Drop -> Close at scope end
    feeds: ["https://blog.rust-lang.org/feed.xml", "https://jet-lang.dev/feed.xml"]

    // async fn fetch(&self, url: &str) -> Result<Feed, fk::Error> becomes a wait point.
    tasks: feeds.map((u) -> task client.fetch(u))
    loop t in tasks {
        feed: t.join()
        print("{feed.title}: {feed.items.len()} items")
    }

    // fn watch(&self, url: &str, f: impl Fn(Item) + Send + 'static) -> WatchHandle
    // Retention: 'static (until the handle drops). Thread: the crate's workers. Delivery: this task.
    seen: shared Counter{seen: 0}
    _watch: client.watch(feeds[1], (item) -> {
        print("new: {item.title}")
        seen.seen += 1                                         // Shared<Counter>: safe from any thread
    }, deliver: .Owner)
    sleep(200ms)
    print("seen {seen.seen}")
}                                                              // the watch closes first, then client
```

```text
Rust Blog: 10 items
Jet: 4 items
new: FFI v3 lands
seen 1
```

`watch` retains the closure, so its captures must be owned or sendable. A plain mutable local would be refused:

```text
Error [E3233]: `watch` keeps this callback after the call returns, so it cannot borrow `counter`.
 Fix: Make it shared: `counter: shared Counter{seen: 0}`.
```

### Best competitor equivalent

```rust
// The closest cross-language equivalent: exposing the same async crate to Python with PyO3, by hand.
#[pyfunction]
fn fetch(py: Python<'_>, url: String) -> PyResult<Bound<'_, PyAny>> {
    pyo3_asyncio::tokio::future_into_py(py, async move { Ok(client().fetch(&url).await?.title) })
}
// The binding author writes this per function; Jet's rustbind writes it from rustdoc JSON.
```

