decisions.explainer:
Plain words first. [Int#4] means a fixed array of exactly four Int slots. A list [Int] can grow and keeps its items in separate heap storage, while a fixed array keeps its slots inline. i -> i * 2 is a small function: given position i, it returns i * 2. A function value can be called any number of times in Jet; there is no call-once kind.

Copy versus own. A plain number can be copied freely. A value that owns a resource, such as an open file or the Slot below, cannot. Its Close step must run exactly once, so four copies would close it four times. That is why filled refuses owning values. from_fn instead makes a fresh, separate owner for every slot.

filled(value) evaluates value once, like any argument, then copies it into every slot. For [Int#0], that one value is still evaluated and then dropped.

from_fn(make) calls make for position 0, 1, 2 and so on, in order, once each. For [Int#0] it never calls make.

When building fails. If make returns an error, from_fn stops. The slots built so far are closed once, newest first, by the existing automatic cleanup. Slots never built are never read or closed. The original error goes back to the caller unchanged. Here build returns the helper's result, and run sees the error:

```jet
#Error
enum OpenError { Full }

struct Slot { id: Int }
impl Slot.Close { fn close(^self) { print("close {self.id}") } }

fn open_slot(i: Int) -> Slot OpenError! {
    if i == 2 -> return Err(OpenError.Full)  // the third slot fails
    return Ok(Slot{id: i})
}

fn build() -> [Slot#4] OpenError! {
    [Slot#4].from_fn(open_slot)              // stops at position 2
}

fn run() {
    if build() == {
        .Ok(_) -> print("built")
        .Err(.Full) -> print("got OpenError.Full")
    }
}
// prints: close 1
//         close 0
