decisions.explainer:
Beginner path: types automatically get every structurally derivable trait whose eligibility rules their fields satisfy, not just four standard traits. Equatable, Debug, Printable, Comparable and eligible encoding traits need no request marker. If fields cannot support a trait, no automatic implementation appears. #!Equatable refuses automatic equality for one type; #Equatable requests it when package defaults are off. A hand-written implementation always wins, even over refusal or package policy. A request still needs eligible fields. Package policy can disable automatic derivation; this ballot changes neither the switch nor its precedence.

Expert path: implement the contract by hand, refuse automatic code, or author a top-level marker. Most markers, including #Test, #Get, #Unsafe, #Inline, #Allow and #Multiversion, have no trait relationship; a trait need not have any marker. A derives clause links one code-writing marker to a trait; it is not a fifth marker kind. Sharing a spelling in A is convenient, not what creates the link.

Complete marker-creation surface, shown once using A. marker is always the declaring word. $sites lists permitted attachment sites; typed parameters are use-site arguments. A body adds code; derives names the contract its members implement. pub exports from a module. Compiler-provided markers are declared in jet_builtins; copying their names cannot grant their authority.

```jet
// Library: rule marker; records a fact, no code-writing body.
marker Reviewed($sites: [.Function, .Method])

// Library: code-writing marker; adds a method, not a trait.
marker DebugText($sites: [.Type]) {
    tname :: target.name
    type_name :: target.$name
    impl $tname { fn debug_string(self) -> String { $type_name } }
}

// Core: pure contract beside an independent deriving marker.
pub trait Equatable { fn equal(self, rhs: Self) -> Bool }
pub marker Equatable($sites: [.Type]) derives Equatable {
    fn equal(self, rhs: Self) -> Bool {
        prep loop field in Self.$fields {
            if self.$field != rhs.$field -> return false
        }
        return true
    }
}

// Library: fixed group, not a new trait or nested marker.
pub marker Model = #[Codable, Equatable, Debug]

// routes.jet: exported library rule marker.
pub marker Get(path: String, cache: Int{0}, $sites: [.Function, .Method])
// app.jet
use "routes.jet" as web
#web.Get("/health")
fn health() -> String { "ok" }

// jet_builtins: trusted compiler-provided gate and role declarations.
pub marker Unsafe(reason: String, obligations: ObligationMode{.None}, $sites: [.Function, .Method, .Block, .Operation])
pub marker Role(role: Role)
// Bundled Core: compiler marker used through the private module.
use jet_builtins as builtin
#builtin.Role(.Add)
pub trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self }
```

Equality here illustrates record fields; preserve the full existing enum and numeric implementations. Role shows its typed declaring signature; its approved attachment restrictions still apply. A gate gets authority from trusted origin and compiler checks, not from lacking a body. Only bundled Core and admitted providers may import jet_builtins; Role itself stays Core-only. Existing public language markers retain their approved use-site spellings.

Groups have no parameters and cannot include gates, refusals, retired markers or other groups. Apply an exported library group through its import name, just like #web.Get. Private markers remain local; pub grants no checking authority.

Expert control, unchanged in every option:
```jet
#!Equatable
struct Handle { fd: Int }
impl Handle.Equatable {
    fn equal(self, rhs: Self) -> Bool { self.fd == rhs.fd }
}
```
The refusal stops automatic code, not hand-written implementation. Shared work: migrate old providers to marker bodies, preserve their generated code and eligibility, and keep one declaration-backed registry. The six-line option examples use ... for the identical equality body above; only the link changes.

