options.technical:
One list. Each entry is an `Output` value from the closed nine-kind set. `outputs:` in package.jet adds entries as data. Each `b.add_*` call in `fn build` adds an entry and returns it, so the `BuildTarget` handle type becomes `Output`. Names are unique across both sources. A duplicate is a registered error naming both lines.

Kinds from code. `b.add_executable` makes an Executable, and the new `b.add_service` makes a Service. `b.add_library` makes a Library, and `b.add_test` makes a Check. `b.add_asset_bundle`, `b.add_doc` and `b.add_package` make a Bundle. `b.add_install` and `b.add_publish` stay steps that act on an output. Environment, Image, System and Fleet stay data only, because `jet env` and deploy read them without running project code.

Fields. A code-made output may pass `entry:`, a checked function reference. Without it, the entry is `fn run` in its first source file, as today. Both forms accept `options:`, the output's own build settings. `b.output(name)` returns a data-made output, so build code can change it with the change calls of D-JAI-CONFIG1. Changes stop when `fn build` returns and Jet seals the plan.

Zero setup and the default. With no runnable output in the list, the root `fn run` is one implicit Executable named after the package. A root `fn run` still runs first for bare `jet run` and `jet dev`; the default picks only when no root `fn run` exists. The default is one settings row: `defaults: { run: cli }` in data, `b.default(cli)` in code, `jet run cli` for one run. `jet dev` uses the `run` default because dev always runs the program.

Selection by name. A word naming a listed output selects it. A path with `/` or ending in `.jet` selects a file. A word naming both a file and an output whose entry is in that file selects that output. Only a word naming two different things is an error.

Commands and listing. `jet build` builds every compiled output and reports them in name order, and `jet build name` builds one. `jet test` runs every Check. `jet inspect build` prints the one list with each output's origin line, settings and default mark. Editors list code-made outputs from the last `jet inspect build`; data outputs need no build run.

Dependencies. `pkgs.dep.name` reads a dependency's data outputs, as today. A dependency's code-made output is reachable through `b.include(dep)`, which already runs that build in a sandbox. Otherwise the error names both fixes.

Worked example, with no root `fn run`:

```
// package.jet
outputs: {
    cli: .Executable{ name: "cli", entry: cli_main }
    api: .Service{ name: "api", entry: serve }
}
defaults: { run: cli }
build: { web: .{ optimize: size, os: web } }

// build.jet
fn build(b: Build) {
    loop name in ["game", "editor"] {
        b.add_executable("{name}_web", ["{name}.jet"], [], options: b.profile("web"))
    }
}

$ jet run            # cli, the default
$ jet dev game_web   # made in build code, same list
$ jet build          # api, cli, editor_web, game_web
$ jet inspect build
api          Service      package.jet:3
cli          Executable   package.jet:2    default
editor_web   Executable   build.jet:3      os web, optimize size
game_web     Executable   build.jet:3      os web, optimize size

error: output `api` is declared twice: package.jet:3 and build.jet:5
  fix: keep one of the two
```

