decisions.detail:
Grounding at dev d9bed7c10c. Line numbers are from that tree. The card scout pass used be55563b38, the reviewers used d42c9655c5 (docs-only changes), and every finding below was re-read at d9bed7c10c.

The collector. sema_user_rule_collect_module (UserTemplates.jet:417-458) handles struct type markers, top-level functions and text blocks only. Function and text block output skips the member shadow check that structs get (:440 and :456, against :391-415). A target record holding a Char fails conversion (:130), and the marker is then skipped by ?? next (:426, :438) with no diagnostic. User derives are struct-only too (:333-338).

Sites today. MARKER_SOURCE_SITES (MarkerSource.jet:9-12) lists eighteen kinds: Package, File, Module, Function, Method, Block, Statement, Expression, Type, Impl, Declaration, Constant, Field, Variant, Parameter, Test, Operation and Text. The site check accepts a declared library marker on any listed kind and otherwise reports E0355. So a body on an enum, variant, method, field, module or package is accepted and never runs. The card asks for struct, enum, variant, fn, method, field, module, package and block.

Records under A. Type gives TypeInfo, whose form says struct, enum or distinct. Variant gives CaseInfo. Function gives FunctionInfo and Method gives MethodInfo. Field gives FieldInfo. Package gives PackageInfo as D-META-REFLECT2=A defines it. Block and Text give the block text as a String, as today. Module needs a new module record, and FieldInfo and CaseInfo need an owner. REFLECT2 has neither, so D-MARKER-REFLECT1 adds them as named amendments to REFLECT2.

Several kinds. When a marker lists more than one kind, target is a closed site value with one case per listed kind, each carrying that kind's record. Its case type is generated from $sites and has no source name. The body matches it with the ordinary if target == { … } form. A missing listed case is an error at the declaration. A marker that lists one kind gets the record directly, so most markers never write a match.

Cost of checking once. Today a body is evaluated per use. Only bindings, if on a known Bool, comptime loops and reject are supported, and anything else is E0956 (Derives.jet:1360-1506; per-use binding at :1554-1572). Option A needs a new static checker for marker bodies plus the anonymous site sum type. PLAN-6039 Stage 4 sizes both and lands the checker before the new site kinds.

Where added items go. Items land in the module that holds the target, beside it. A module target receives items inside itself, and a package target receives them in its root module. A field, variant or method target adds members only through an explicit impl block naming the owner. The existing rule still refuses changing or shadowing a written member.

A field-site body, for example:
```jet
marker Audited($sites: [.Field]) {
    owner :: target.$owner.$name
    getter :: "audited_{target.$name}"
    impl $owner {
        fn $getter(self) -> Bool { true }
    }
}
```

Order. A body reads written declarations only, never another marker's added items, so the order of markers cannot change meaning. Added items are then checked like written code. Two markers adding the same name is the existing collision error, which names both origins.

Edge cases. The nine kinds without records are File, Impl, Declaration, Constant, Test, Statement, Expression, Parameter and Operation. A body that lists any of them is an error at the declaration naming the nine kinds that run bodies; markers without a body may still use them. A negated use runs nothing. Functions inside inline modules are targets, so the collector walks nested modules. A wrap(call) statement is legal only in a Function or Method arm.

Fixed by ratified decisions. D-META-USER1=A gives a library rule a body that adds code. D-MARKER-LAW1=A lets package markers read facts about their target, add ordinary items and reject. D-MARKER-DECL1=A (2026-10-06) also keeps wrap(call) as a marker-body statement. On a function target it wraps the definition, so direct calls and method values run the wrapper.

A conflict this ballot does not settle. D-META-COMPILER1=A says wrapping a declaration becomes a marker plus rewrite_body, and its amendment list omits DECL1. The shipped predecessor, policy … { wrap(call) }, is parsed at Parser/Items.jet:166-190. PLAN-6039 carries the reconciliation as its own item. Under every option here, wrap(call) stays where DECL1 put it.

Spelling. D-MARKER-SITES1=B, respelled by D-DECL-META1=A, writes the site list as $sites. D-MARKER-MODULE1=A qualifies library markers through the import name. This ballot decides only what the body receives at each site and when it is checked.

Pillars. Pillar 2, Easy To Reason About: a reader of the declaration knows every record its body can see, and a wrong read fails there. Pillar 4, Ladders, Not Cliffs: under A, moving a marker from functions to modules adds one arm on the same rung. Under C it is a cliff, because modules and fields must move to the build function.

Peer languages. Rust attribute macros get a token stream and parse it, so any kind can arrive and mistakes show per use. A Swift peer macro tests the node kind inside the macro. C# source generators and Java annotation processors receive typed nodes or elements and test their kind. Kotlin KSP offers typed declarations with a visitor. Option A gives one checked arm per kind before any use, which is better for reasoning than all of these.

Python decorators receive the function or class object at run time and may do anything, so the marker tells a reader little. Zig comptime switches on a type's info, which is closest to option A, but Zig checks a branch only when it is used. Elixir macros receive quoted code and fail per use, like option B.

Beginner path: write a marker with one site kind and a body, and use target directly; a typo fails at the declaration. Expert path: list several kinds, match on target, and add owner members with an impl block. Under B a library can ship arms that fail only in users' builds. Under C a module-wide marker needs the build function instead.

The shared program. Every option of all five ballots is measured against it:

```jet
// routes.jet, in package routes (the library)
diagnostic BadPath(path: String,
    $what: "route `{path}` must start with /",
    $why: "routes are matched from the site root",
    $fix: "write \"/{path}\"")
marker Route(path: String, $sites: [.Module, .Function]) {
    if !path.starts_with("/") -> reject(BadPath(path: path))
    tag :: if target == {
        .Module(m) -> "mod_{m.$name}"
        .Function(f) -> "route_{f.$name}"
    }
    fn $tag() -> String { $path }
}
```
```jet
// app.jet, in package app, whose package.jet lists routes as a dependency
use routes as web
#web.Route("/users")
module users {
    #web.Route("/list") fn list() -> String { "ada" }
    #web.Route("list") fn count() -> Int { 1 }
}
struct User { name: String, id: Int }
fn run() { print(users.list()) }
```

The use on count fires: Error [routes.BadPath] at app.jet:6, because "list" has no leading slash. The other two uses add mod_users (from app.jet:3) and route_list (from app.jet:5) inside module users. Each ballot changes only the lines its options name. The diagnostic declaration and reject line use the diagnostics ballot's recommended form, and the match uses the site ballot's.

Ground rules for the code. No line ends with a semicolon, which is E0373 under D-SEMI1=A. Splices use only template bindings, as in fn $tag and impl $owner. No declaration writes pub, which D-VIS-MODULE1 removed. The library is its own package, so its diagnostic codes carry the package name whatever alias an application chooses.

